<worksheet>
    <title>Lab: Heat Visualization</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-1-dimension">
        <title>One Dimension</title>

    </subsection>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Let's visualize worksheet 13 and see what is actually going on with
            our solution to the heat equation.
        </p>

        <p>
            For each one of these problems, we have a beam of length <m>5</m>,
            and we have the same properties of the materials to give use
            <m>\alpha=4</m>.
            Those are fixed, and we look at the effect of changing other aspects
            of the problem.
        </p>

        <p>
            Run the code cell below to set up the animation.
            It will print "Done!" when it has finished.
            You want to make sure you evaluate this initial cell, so that the
            necessary libraries and functions are loaded and defined.
            Then continue on to run animations for the problems from the
            previous worksheet.
            (Note, some of the code may take a bit to run, "patience young
            grasshopper!")
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <sage language="sage">
        <input>
            import numpy as np
            import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
            import matplotlib.animation as animation
            from matplotlib.animation import FuncAnimation
            from matplotlib import rc
            rc('animation', html='jshtml')

            fig, ax = plt.subplots()

            def animate(i):
                ax.clear()
                ax.plot(x,u(x,i*.1)) # Plot the curve
                ax.set_xlabel('x')
                ax.set_ylabel('u(x,t)')
                ax.annotate('Time t = '+str(i*.1), (-1,ylim[1]),color='red',size = 14)
                ax.set_xlim([-1,6])
                ax.set_ylim(ylim)
                #ax.set_aspect('equal', adjustable='box')
            print('Done!')
        </input>
    </sage>

    <exercise workspace="0cm">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The code below shows the solution to problem 2:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp = \alpha \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(5,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(x,0) \amp = x^2 - 5x </mrow>
                </md>
                Run the code to animate the solution over time.
                Then think about the following questions...
            </p>
            <sage language="sage">
                <input>
                    def u(x,t):
                        N=range(1,10)
                        u_n=[]
                        for n in N:
                            u_n.append(100*(-1+(-1)**n)/(n**3*np.pi**3)*np.exp(-4*(np.pi/5)**2*t)*np.sin(np.pi*x/5))
                        return np.sum(u_n,axis=0)

                    ylim=[-8,1]
                    x=np.linspace(0,5,100)

                    ani = FuncAnimation(fig, animate, frames=25, interval=500, repeat=False)
                    ani

                </input>
            </sage>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What's the only difference between this problem and the
                    previous problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does the solution look like <m>u(x,0)</m> initially?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens to the curve over time?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Where does all the heat go over time?
                    Why?
                    What happens to the endpoints?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0cm">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The code below shows the solution to problem 3:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp = 4 \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(5,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(x,0) \amp = x </mrow>
                </md>
                Run the code to animate the solution over time.
                Then think about the following questions...
            </p>
            <sage language="sage">
                <input>
                    def u(x,t):
                        N=range(1,30)
                        u_n=[]
                        for n in N:
                            u_n.append(10*(-1+(-1)**n)/(n**2*np.pi**2)*np.exp(-4*(np.pi/5)**2*t)*np.cos(np.pi*x/5))
                        return np.sum(u_n,axis=0)+5/2

                    ylim=[-1,6]
                    x=np.linspace(0,5,100)

                    ani = FuncAnimation(fig, animate, frames=25, interval=500, repeat=False)
                    ani

                </input>
            </sage>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What's the big difference with the bounds of this problem
                    and the previous problem?
                    <em>These bounds mean the beam is insulated at both ends.</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Does the solution look like <m>u(x,0)</m> initially?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens to the bounds over time?
                    Does this mess with the boundary conditions?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens to the curve over time?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Why doesn't the heat go to 0 over time like in previous
                    problems?
                    Where does the heat go?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

